<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip de Montmorency, Count of Horn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1524">1524</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Count of Hoorn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip de Montmorency (1524–1568), Count of Horne, was a nobleman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, admiral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and a knight of the Golden Fleece. With Egmont and William of Orange he led the noble opposition to Philip II's religious policy. Arrested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> in 1567, he was beheaded with Egmont on the Grand Place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 5 June 1568.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-de-montmorency-count-of-horn.jpg" alt="Philip de Montmorency, Count of Horn" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gysius-Oorsprong-en-voortgang_9090.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was the eldest of the four children of Josef van Montmorency, Count of Nevele, and Anna van Egmont the Elder, who lived at Ooidonk Castle. His father died early in 1530 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, where he had gone for the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. His mother then married Johan II, Count of Horn, one of the wealthiest nobles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, who in 1540 left the County of Horne to her children on condition that they took his name. Philip served Charles V as page and later chamberlain, and in 1546 married Walburgis van Nieuwenaer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He became stadtholder of Guelders in 1555. In 1559 he commanded the fleet that carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and he remained at the Spanish court until 1563. On his return he joined William, Prince of Orange, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamoral-count-of-egmont">Lamoral, Count of Egmont</a>, at the head of the faction that opposed the imposition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> by Cardinal Granvelle and in the end forced his resignation. After Granvelle's departure the three went on resisting the Inquisition and Spanish rule.</p><p>In April 1566 the Council of State sent Horn's younger brother, Floris of Montmorency, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in a last attempt to avoid war, but Floris was placed under house arrest. Philip II, though he seemed to give way, had resolved to punish his opponents, and replaced the regent, Margaret, Duchess of Parma, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> at the head of a veteran army. Orange fled, but Egmont and Horn ignored his warning and stayed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Both were tried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-troubles">Council of Troubles</a> and condemned as traitors. Efforts to obtain Horn a fair trial failed, though rulers across the continent appealed for clemency on his behalf, and he was beheaded with Egmont before the Town Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. Two years later Philip II had Floris strangled in secret and spread the rumour that he had died of disease. Both counts remained faithful Catholics. They are usually named together, as Egmond en Hoorne, as the first leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> before William of Orange, and a statue on the Square du Petit Sablon in Brussels commemorates them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip de Montmorency, Count of Horn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Philip de Montmorency</b>, count of Horne, an admiral and knight of the Golden Fleece, joined the noble opposition to Philip II's policies in the Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-horne">County of Horne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Grand-Place, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.35&amp;year=1552" aria-label="Grand-Place on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Philip de Montmorency, Count of Horn"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 5772h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 5772h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.35&amp;year=1552">What was here in 1552? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
